  interestingFactsEn: "Although his biography is not formally full of household details, Brahmagupta's life and scientific career are full of surprising, and at times paradoxical, facts, some of which reveal him as a unique and controversial figure in the history of science.\n\n♪ Personality, full of contradictions: The academic and theology, perhaps the most interesting aspect of Brahmagupta's personality, is his amazing duality. ♪ Scientific innovator vs. Religious Conservator: On the one hand, he made a breakthrough in mathematics, formalizing the rules of action with zero and negative numbers and actually predisposing the notion of gravity.On the other hand, he was a deeply religious Shivait and had a very conservative position in astronomy, and Ariabhata's violent criticism: his main opponent was the great predecessor of Ariabhat, and Brahmagupta sharply criticized the idea of spinning the Earth, calling those who stood by it \"outcasts of the sphere\" (`gola-bāhyena'), that is, people expelled from the circle of respected scientists.He said, \"O my Lord, I am not one of those who are in need of guidance.\"\n\nAstronomy: Gravity and the shape of the Earth Contrary to its conservative views on eclipses, Brahmagupta made several discoveries that were centuries ahead of his time. The intuition of gravity: He was the first Indian scientist to describe gravity as a force of gravity using the term \"gurutvakarshane\".In his work, he explained the fall of bodies to the Earth by the fact that it was built in the nature of the earth — to attract things like the nature of water — to flow. Sharolike Earth: He had been bold in saying that the earth and the sky were spherical in shape, and even in saying that the earth was moving. To criticism that stones and trees would fall from a balloon earth, he said that all heavy objects were drawn to the center of the earth, so people stood on it from every side.\n\n♪ Mathematical innovations: Zero and poetry of numbers His mathematical achievements are striking not only in their depths but also in their form. Zero as a full number: Brahmagupta was the first to begin to see zero as a full number participating in the calculations, not as a symbol of void. He gave clear arithmetical rules with zero, though he was wrong, claiming that `00.0 = 0'.Mathematics in poems: Brahmagupt wrote his scientific tracts in poem form, which was a common practice in ancient India, which gave him a poetic sound of poetry, but unfortunately he did not provide proof of his theorem, only with ready results and algorithms.The discovery collection: His contribution is so great that his name is given to several mathematical objects: the Brahmagupta formula (for the area of the written quadrilateral), the Brahmagupta theorem, the identity of Brahmagupta-Fibonacci and the interpolation formula.\n\n♪ Recognition and legacy of the Titul \"Gemma Mathematics\": Bhaskar II's late great mathematician was so enthralled by Brahmagupta's intelligence that he was awarded the title \"Ganita Chakra Chudamani\" - \"The value of the circle of mathematicians.\" Impact on the world: It was through translations of his works into Arabic (in particular, \"Brahma-Sphota-Siddhanty\") that the Islamic world became acquainted with the Indian position system of numeracy.This knowledge later reached Europe through Arabs, with a tremendous impact on the development of the entire world's mathematics.\n\nThe result of the exercise in the table is that Ariabhata was criticized for astronomy, but recognized its genius in later works. ♪ Astronomy pre-empted gravity theory by calling it the force of gravity of the Earth.His works were written in poem form and did not provide proof. ♪ The legacy ♪ was called \"The Value of Mathematics Circle.\" His works were translated into Arabic and Asian, which changed the course of the world's mathematics."
